    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fort Hunter Liggett, named in 1941 after General Hunter Liggett, began as a 200,000‑acre training ground for the U.S. Army.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://home.army.mil/liggett/about/history",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Today the post covers 167,000 acres, making it the largest United States Army Reserve installation in the country.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Hunter_Liggett",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The fort’s rugged terrain hosts live‑fire exercises and large‑scale field maneuvers that test soldiers in realistic combat scenarios.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Hunter_Liggett",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Movie buffs will recognize the old Bailey Bridge over the Nacimiento River, which served as the opening helicopter scene in *We Were Soldiers*.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fort_Hunter_Liggett",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The historic Hacienda, once a ranchhouse, now welcomes guests and has even housed the Installation Commander’s Quarters during certain periods.",
